<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Curonians</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 1267 CE (approx.) · capital Apuolė</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Seafaring Baltic people of the coast of Courland, feared as pirates across the Baltic, who resisted the Livonian crusade until 1267.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Curonians lived along the Baltic coast of what is now western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Latvia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimbert">Rimbert's</a> life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansgar">Ansgar</a> describes a Swedish king attacking their stronghold at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apuole">Apuolė</a> in 854, and they appear in Norse sagas as both trading partners and raiders. Their land was divided into several districts, each with its own hillforts and leaders.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They combined farming and fishing with seaborne trade and plunder, raiding Danish and Swedish coasts in the 11th to 13th centuries. Their cremation burials and weapons resemble those of Scandinavians. In 1230 a ruler called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamekins">Lamekins</a> made a treaty with the papal legate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-of-alna">Baldwin of Alna</a>, accepting baptism in return for keeping his lands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-order">Livonian Order</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bishopric-of-courland">Bishopric of Courland</a> conquered them between 1240 and 1267, despite a great uprising after the Order's defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-durbe">Durbe</a> in 1260. A small group of Curonian freemen, the 'Curonian kings', kept a special status into modern times, and the people merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/curonians.jpg" alt="Curonians" width="320" height="514"><figcaption>Image: A. Švābe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OldCourland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 1267 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Apuolė</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lamekins</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Baltic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Curonian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>, <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Couronia, Kursa, Cori</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=934&amp;focus=curonians" aria-label="Curonians on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5150 264 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Curonians lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5470 520h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5470 520h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Apuolė. <a href="/atlas?year=934&amp;focus=curonians">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

